{{- /*
model.leg.sim — the body behind the row claiming an acknowledged write
survives losing the process.

A drawn interleaving of writes, reads and crash points, with every read
judged against what the writes acknowledged. No model to select and no
reference to pick, which is what makes this the shortest leg in the
tier: the subject is held to its own acknowledgements, and the only
thing the schedule needs from the interface is the pair of verbs, the
two pools they draw from, and the projection saying which key a write
lands on.

The three type arguments are spelled rather than inferred. The oracle is
a map from the key to the record, and Go infers neither from a closure —
so the schedule names the subject, the key and the value, in that order.
*/ -}}
{{- define "model.leg.sim" -}}
{{ if .Fault -}}
// {{ .Assert }} drives the same schedule with the medium free to fail:
// partway through, the subject is put into the state where its writes
// report {{ renderExpr .Fault }}, and every later write is refused by
// the subject itself.
//
// The other half of the crash claim. Its sibling asks what an
// acknowledged write survives; this asks what a REFUSED one left behind,
// and nothing else in the run ever refuses one.
//
// Induced rather than intercepted, which is what makes it falsifiable. A
// double answering the error in front of the subject never delivers the
// write, so the subject cannot have written anything down and the claim
// has nothing to be false about. This hands it the write and lets it
// fail on its own terms — which is where a store that records before it
// commits is caught.
{{- else -}}
// {{ .Assert }} drives a drawn schedule of writes, reads and crashes,
// and asks whether the medium still owes what the writes promised.
//
// A claim no other leg in this file can state. The differential compares
// two live instances and the laws hold after each step of one; this is
// about what is left when an instance stops existing mid-run, which is a
// question only the medium can answer.
//
// The engine owns the interleaving, the shrinking and the oracle. This
// supplies the verbs and the two pools they draw from.
{{- end }}
func {{ .Assert }}(
	tb {{ renderExpr (external "testing" "TB") }},
	sub {{ renderExpr (external .Vocab "Subject") }}[{{ .Subject }}],
{{- if .Draws }}
	{{ .Fixture }} {{ .FixtureType }},
{{- end }}
) {
	tb.Helper()
	{{ if .Fault }}{{ renderExpr (external .LegsPkg "RecoverFaulting") }}{{ else }}{{ renderExpr (external .LegsPkg "Recover") }}{{ end }}[{{ .Subject }}, {{ renderType .Key }}, {{ renderType .Value }}](tb, sub,
		{{ renderExpr (external .CrashPkg "Schedule") }}[{{ .Subject }}, {{ renderType .Key }}, {{ renderType .Value }}]{
			Values: {{ .ValuesFunc }}({{ template "model.leg.args" . }}),
			Keys:   {{ .KeysFunc }}({{ template "model.leg.args" . }}),
			KeyOf:  {{ .KeyOfName }},
			Write: func(ctx {{ renderExpr (external "context" "Context") }}, world {{ .Subject }}, v {{ renderType .Value }}) error {
				return {{ template "model.leg.sim.iface" . }}.{{ .Writer.Method }}({{ if .Writer.TakesCtx }}ctx, {{ end }}v)
			},
			Read: func(ctx {{ renderExpr (external "context" "Context") }}, world {{ .Subject }}, k {{ renderType .Key }}) ({{ renderType .Value }}, error) {
				return {{ template "model.leg.sim.iface" . }}.{{ .Reader.Method }}({{ if .Reader.TakesCtx }}ctx, {{ end }}k)
			},
			Equal:  func(a, b {{ renderType .Value }}) bool { return a == b },
			Absent: {{ template "model.leg.sim.absent" . }},
		}{{ if .Fault }}, {{ renderExpr .Fault }}{{ end }})
}
{{- end -}}

{{- /*
model.leg.sim.iface narrows the harness's subject type to the
declaration's own before naming a method on it.

The schedule is typed at the subject the harness spells, and the methods
belong to the interface — the same split every action closure carries.
Where the two are already the same type the conversion is noise, so it
is only rendered where the leg knows a distinct interface type.
*/ -}}
{{- define "model.leg.sim.iface" -}}
{{- if .Iface }}{{ renderType .Iface }}(world){{ else }}world{{ end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /*
model.leg.sim.absent renders how a read reports a key that is not there:
the declaration's own sentinel where a mixin stamps one, and nil
otherwise — which the schedule reads as "any error means absent".

The lenient form is not a shortcut. A contract that names no miss
sentinel has not said which error means absent, and insisting on one
would red every implementation that reports it its own way.
*/ -}}
{{- define "model.leg.sim.absent" -}}
{{- if .Miss }}{{ renderExpr (external .CrashPkg "Is") }}({{ renderExpr .Miss }}){{ else }}nil{{ end -}}
{{- end -}}
